The batch_edit_translations function appears to be a critical security vulnerability as it is exposing sensitive data. It contains a potential leakage of sensitive user information due to its unauthenticated access, which could potentially reveal confidential system information or personal details of users who are not supposed to have this level of visibility.

Moreover, the function directly accepts POSTed data without any sanitisation or input validation, which makes it susceptible to injection attacks. It should validate and sanitize all user inputs prior to using them in database queries to prevent SQL Injection vulnerability (CWE-89). 

Furthermore, this function performs operations based on the results of a POST request without any authentication checks. The use of @login_required decorator ensures that only authenticated users can access this view but it doesn't confirm if the user has the necessary permissions to perform the operation. If an unauthorised user is able to impersonate another, they might be able to edit translations on behalf of other users leading to severe security issues (CWE-89).

Lastly, error handling should be more robust in this function. Current implementation only returns HttpResponseBadRequest or HttpResponseForbidden when there are errors. If a potential attacker can provide input that causes an exception within the function but without returning any HTTP response status codes, it will remain undetected by security scanners and potentially allow for unauthorized execution of arbitrary code on the server (CWE-79).
</analysis>

