<analysis>
The function delete contains a potential SQL injection vulnerability. The '@jwt_required' decorator in the code implies that this function is part of an API and might be accessible to users who are not supposed to have such access, including unauthorized users or even malicious entities. If the user ID supplied by these unauthorized users comes from a source where it has been passed through HTML encoding (like '<' becomes '&lt;'), then this function can be potentially exploited with an attacker crafted payload to perform SQL injection attacks.

SQL injections are possible because the function directly concatenates user input into the SQL query string without any sanitisation or parameterisation. An example of a maliciously constructed payload could look like "' OR '1'='1' --" which makes the entire statement always true, effectively disabling the WHERE clause and deleting all users from the database. 

However, as this code is using string formatting ('f-string') to build SQL query it seems that the input user_id should be safe (assuming it comes from a trusted source). If it doesn't, then even if an attacker managed to bypass authentication and get the JWT token, they would still face this injection vulnerability.
</analysis>
 
