In the provided code, there's a potential SQL injection vulnerability. The function is directly concatenating user input into an SQL query without proper sanitisation or parameterisation. This could allow for unauthorised data manipulation and exploitation if malicious input is sent to `id` field of the database table.

The SQL command has no placeholders (i.e., parameters) in it, so even though there's some basic SQL-like syntax used here, an attacker could still exploit this by inserting more complex SQL statements into the ID parameter which would be interpreted as part of the query string and not a separate parameter to be interpolated.

Also, no error handling is done when fetching data from database. If there's any issue with the fetch operation (like connection errors), it won’t be captured by this function resulting in unhandled exceptions. 

