<analysis>
The provided code snippet contains a potential security vulnerability related to SQL Injection. The issue arises from the way the `id` is processed and used in the SQL query. If there is any user input that gets passed into the function, it may be possible for an attacker to manipulate this user input to cause unexpected behavior or even execute arbitrary SQL commands as part of a potential SQL injection attack.

This can occur because Python's `%` operator (used in string formatting) can lead to unintended side effects if misused, leading to security vulnerabilities like SQL Injection. Here, it is used directly with the table name and metadata type without any sanitisation or parameterization, which makes it susceptible to injection attacks.

The `idNormalizer` check also seems unnecessary as it's being overwritten by an assignment in a subsequent if-elif clause when `type(id) == unicode:` is True. The else part of the condition can be removed without affecting security, but for completeness, we will point out that this could potentially cause problems later on if other code changes are made to handle non-string types for ids.

Lastly, it's worth noting that there seems to be a missing exception handling in case an update fails. This can make debugging more difficult when something goes wrong with the database operations. It would be best practice to catch and handle potential exceptions during these operations. 
</analysis>

