The provided code contains a potential SQL injection vulnerability and an unprotected storage of sensitive data (user IDs in this case). 

In the provided function, SQL command construction is being performed by concatenation with user-provided input without any parameterised query or prepared statement. This makes it vulnerable to SQL injection attacks. An attacker could leverage this vulnerability to manipulate the SQL command to retrieve or modify sensitive data from the database. 

Moreover, the code directly commits changes to the database without any sort of checking. If an error occurs during execution, these changes will be lost. This is a serious issue known as "lost updates" and can lead to inconsistencies between the application's state and its persisted data.  

The user ID being stored in plain text (session) might expose sensitive information if intercepted. It should ideally be hashed or encrypted before storing, preferably using a secure library for this purpose. 

In conclusion, given these issues the confidence level of these vulnerabilities is "High". SQL Injection (CWE-89) and Unprotected Storage of Credentials (CWE-256).
</analysis>

